  <h3>Gaps and quirks, disclosed</h3>
  <ul class="offerings spaced-bottom">
    <li>No OAIC report was published for January to June 2025, when a dashboard replaced the report cycle. That half stays a gap.</li>
    <li>No whole-period timeliness figure is published for 2024, so that stretch of the trend stays a gap.</li>
    <li>In the latest workbook, source-of-breach rows sum to 673 against a published total of 670. The difference is disclosed, not reallocated.</li>
    <li>Earlier reports count multi-entity breaches differently, so the history marks where comparisons stop being like-for-like.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="NDB headline figures" tabindex="0">
    <table class="ruled">
      <caption>Headline figures, 1 July to 31 December 2025</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Figure</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">670</th><td>Notifications received, up 12.6% on 595 in the same half of 2024</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">60.4%</th><td>Notifications where a malicious or criminal attack was the source</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">37.8%</th><td>Cyber incidents</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">29%</th><td>Human error</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">5.2%</th><td>System faults</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">58.7%</th><td>Breaches identified within 10 days (349 of 595 in-range breaches)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">50.6%</th><td>Breaches notified to the OAIC within 30 days (308 of 609 in-range breaches)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="table-note">Cyber incidents (253 of 670) are one part of malicious or criminal attacks (405 of 670), alongside social engineering or impersonation (88), rogue employee or insider threat (34), and theft of paperwork or storage devices (30). Source: OAIC NDB statistics via data.gov.au, as computed by NDB Insights.</p>
</section>

<section class="band-mist" id="trust" aria-labelledby="trust-title">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust and security</p>
      <h2 id="trust-title">Aggregate data only, with set limits on the chat</h2>
    </div>
    <dl class="facts facts--icons">
      <div><dt class="ic ic--database">Data used</dt><dd>Aggregate, published statistics from the OAIC. No breach, entity or individual is identified.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--slash">Chat limits</dt><dd>Refuses legal advice, questions about whether a specific incident is an eligible data breach, and anything that would identify a breach, an entity or an individual.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--bar">Figures</dt><dd>Computed by the platform from pinned sources. The model never writes a digit.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--check">Integrity checks</dt><dd>At start-up, facts are re-summed against hand-verified published totals and the pinned workbook hash. A mismatch stops the service.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--history">Restatements</dt><dd>The latest published value is shown with the first-published value beside it, and the restating report is named.</dd></div>
      </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <div data-acc-scope>
    <div class="acc-head">
      <h2 id="questions-title">Common questions</h2>
      <button type="button" class="btn-text" data-expand-all hidden><span class="js-label">Expand all</span><span class="visually-hidden"> questions</span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="points">
      <details id="q-ndb-legal"><summary><span class="acc-title">Does it give legal advice?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>No. It answers questions about the published statistics, not about your obligations under the Privacy Act, and it won't say whether a specific incident is an eligible data breach.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ndb-overlap"><summary><span class="acc-title">Why do 60.4% and 37.8% both describe attacks?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>The OAIC's "malicious or criminal attack" category includes cyber incidents, rogue employees and insider threats, social engineering or impersonation, and theft of paperwork or storage devices. In July to December 2025, cyber incidents made up 253 of the 405 malicious or criminal notifications.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ndb-gap"><summary><span class="acc-title">Why is January to June 2025 missing?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>The OAIC published no report for that half: its statistics dashboard replaced the report cycle. Rather than read values off a chart, the platform shows the period as a disclosed gap.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ndb-current"><summary><span class="acc-title">How current is the data?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>The latest published period, July to December 2025, is on the site from the OAIC workbook as at 29 June 2026. Coverage was last checked on 12 September 2026.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ndb-api"><summary><span class="acc-title">Can we use the figures in our own tools?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>Yes. A read-only JSON API returns the same platform-computed figures and facts, and never a number the platform did not compute from the pinned sources.</p></div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

